WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.690
Which outside apps can sign in to
our Microsoft 365 without anyone

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.000
typing a password?

00:00:07.355 --> 00:00:09.845
On 16 October 2026,

00:00:10.195 --> 00:00:15.735
ConnectWise reported unauthorized access
to the app credentials of its Cloud Backup

00:00:15.735 --> 00:00:16.465
service.

00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:18.175
Three questions matter.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.560
First, whose key opens the door?

00:00:21.835 --> 00:00:25.405
Someone with those credentials
signs in as the app itself.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.575
There is no user and
no multi-factor prompt.

00:00:28.925 --> 00:00:31.055
Once an administrator approves an app,

00:00:31.265 --> 00:00:34.095
it keeps its rights until
someone removes them.

00:00:34.295 --> 00:00:39.245
Microsoft reports one management app
that gave itself Global Administrator.

00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:42.253
Second, how fast do they move?

00:00:43.223 --> 00:00:47.773
Microsoft says the attackers used an
open-source framework of AI agents.

00:00:48.093 --> 00:00:51.983
They tried to sign in to
more than 2,250 tenants.

00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:56.543
In several cases, privileged access
came in less than three hours.

00:00:56.873 --> 00:00:58.723
A monthly review is too slow.

00:00:59.013 --> 00:01:03.003
Consider alerts on new app keys
and admin roles, day and night.

00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:06.867
Third, what if our backup
supplier is the incident?

00:01:07.187 --> 00:01:11.317
ConnectWise suspended the backup service,
and two more as a precaution.

00:01:11.807 --> 00:01:15.417
It says existing archives
remain encrypted and preserved.

00:01:15.812 --> 00:01:20.742
On 23 October 2026,
it had given no firm date to return.

00:01:21.032 --> 00:01:25.242
If an MSP runs our IT,
did they use these tools for us?

00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:27.493
What good looks like.

00:01:27.838 --> 00:01:33.188
NIS2 Article 21 requires supply
chain security and access control.

00:01:33.443 --> 00:01:37.253
That means a list of every outside
app with its rights and an owner.

00:01:37.573 --> 00:01:39.663
Logs searched by application ID.

00:01:40.068 --> 00:01:41.818
Alerts handled within the hour.

00:01:42.108 --> 00:01:44.678
And a second copy of
critical mail and files.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:47.840
A suggested first step.

00:01:48.125 --> 00:01:50.935
Ask which outside apps can
read our mail and files,

00:01:51.325 --> 00:01:52.835
or change admin roles.

00:01:53.215 --> 00:01:56.925
Ask whether anyone would notice
a new admin role within hours.

00:01:57.305 --> 00:02:00.015
Ask for a written tenant
review from our MSP.

00:02:00.465 --> 00:02:03.715
Keep the affected apps disabled,
rather than deleted,

00:02:03.965 --> 00:02:06.235
until ConnectWise gives instructions.

00:02:08.017 --> 00:02:10.507
This attack used trust
we had already given.

00:02:10.807 --> 00:02:12.637
Trim what outside apps can do.

